_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 38
---
order: 38
---
short_label: X.38
---
statement: If two medial straight lines commensurable in square only and containing a medial rectangle are added together, then the whole is irrational; let it be called the <i>second bimedial</i> straight line.
---
proof:

Let two medial straight lines *AB* and *BC* commensurable in square only and containing a medial rectangle be added together.

I say that *AC* is irrational.

Set out a rational straight line *DE,* and apply parallelogram *DF* to *DE* equal to the square on *AC,* producing *DG* as breadth. [!just I.41]

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="250" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.38 diagram" src="propX38.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.38",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,40;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,40;0;0",
            "D0;point;free;40,-1000;0;0",
            "D3;point;free;40,1000;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,40,0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,140,0",
            "C;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,180,0",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "D;point;lineSlider;D0,D3,0,90",
            "E;point;lineSlider;D0,D3,0,210",
            "DG';line;perpendicular;D,E;0;0;0",
            "G';point;last;DG';0;0",
            "G;point;proportion;D,E,A,C,A,C,D,G'",
            "F;point;parallelogram;E,D,G",
            "DEFG;polygon;quadrilateral;D,E,F,G;0;0;black;random",
            "H';point;proportion;D,E,A,B,A,B,D,G;0;0",
            "H;point;proportion;D,E,B,C,B,C,H',G",
            "EH;polygon;parallelogram;E,D,H;0;0;black;random"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since the square on *AC* equals the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC* and twice the rectangle *AB* by *BC,* apply *EH,* equal to the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC,* to *DE.* Then the remainder *HF* equals twice the rectangle *AB* by *BC.* [!just II.4]

Since each of the straight lines *AB* and *BC* is medial, therefore the squares on *AB* and *BC* are also medial. But, by hypothesis, twice the rectangle *AB* by *BC* is also medial. And *EH* equals the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC,* while *FH* equals twice the rectangle *AB* by *BC,* therefore each of the rectangles *EH* and *HF* is medial.

And they are applied to the rational straight line *DE,* therefore each of the straight lines *DH* and *HG* is rational and incommensurable in length with *DE.* [!just X.22]

Since *AB* is incommensurable in length with *BC,* and *AB* is to *BC* as the square on *AB* is to the rectangle *AB* by *BC,* therefore the square on *AB* is incommensurable with the rectangle *AB* by *BC.* [!just X.11]

But the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC* is commensurable with the square on *AB,* and twice the rectangle *AB* by *BC* is commensurable with the rectangle *AB* by *BC.* [!just X.15; X.6]

Therefore the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC* is incommensurable with twice the rectangle *AB* by *BC.* [!just X.13]

But *EH* equals the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC,* and *HF* equals twice the rectangle *AB* by *BC.*

Therefore *EH* is incommensurable with *HF,* so that *DH* is also incommensurable in length with *HG.* [!just VI.1; X.11]

Therefore *DH* and *HG* are rational straight lines commensurable in square only, so that *DG* is irrational. [!just X.36]

But *DE* is rational, and the rectangle contained by an irrational and a rational straight line is irrational, therefore the area *DF* is irrational, and the side of the square equal to it is irrational. <div class="just">cf. <a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX20/">X.20</a><br><a href="/elements/books/bookX/definitions/defX.I.4/">X.Def.I.4</a></div>

But *AC* is the side of the square equal to *DF,* therefore *AC* is irrational. Let it be called a *second bimedial* straight line.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

This proposition is used in @X.44, @X.56, @X.62, and @X.67.
